.TH ulockstat 8  "2026-09-19" "USER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
ulockstat \- Summarize user-space mutex lock contention. Uses Linux eBPF/bcc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ulockstat [\-h] [\-p PID] [\-t TID] [\-d DURATION] [\-i INTERVAL] [\-n COUNT] [\-s DEPTH] [\-m MIN_US]
.SH DESCRIPTION
ulockstat traces pthread mutex lock events and displays lock statistics,
using the USDT probes that glibc's NPTL provides. It displays the following
data:

.nf
     PID      Mutex (hex)   Avg Wait   Count   Max Wait  Total Wait
  --------------------------------------------------------------------
   12345       55f3e4a2c010        12.4      31       88.1       384.4

     PID      Mutex (hex)   Avg Hold   Count   Max Hold  Total Hold
  --------------------------------------------------------------------
   12345       55f3e4a2c010        45.2      31      120.3      1401.2
.fi

The first block shows the lock acquisition (wait) data: how long threads
blocked waiting to acquire each mutex.

  'PID'          - process the mutex belongs to
  'Mutex (hex)'  - address of the mutex
  'Avg Wait'     - average time to acquire the mutex
  'Count'        - number of acquisitions that had to wait
  'Max Wait'     - maximum time to acquire the mutex
  'Total Wait'   - total time spent acquiring the mutex

The second block shows the lock holding data: how long each mutex was held.

  'PID'          - process the mutex belongs to
  'Mutex (hex)'  - address of the mutex
  'Avg Hold'     - average time the mutex was held
  'Count'        - number of times the mutex was held (always all of them)
  'Max Hold'     - maximum time the mutex was held
  'Total Hold'   - total time the mutex was held

Times are in microseconds. Below each wait row the stack trace of the call
site that blocked on the lock is printed, which shows where the contention
is created.

This works by attaching USDT probes to the glibc mutex_entry,
mutex_acquired, and mutex_release probe points. The time between
mutex_entry and mutex_acquired is the wait time, and the time between
mutex_acquired and mutex_release is the hold time.

Since this instruments every lock and unlock, it can add noticeable
overhead on a process with a high lock rate. Use it for short periods of
investigation.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux with eBPF and BCC, and a glibc that was built with USDT (SystemTap
probe) support. Up to glibc 2.33 the probes use the "libpthread" provider,
starting with glibc 2.34 they use the "libc" provider.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-h
Print usage message.
.TP
\-p PID
Trace this process ID only.
.TP
\-t TID
Trace this thread ID only.
.TP
\-d DURATION
Total duration of trace in seconds.
.TP
\-i INTERVAL
Print summary at this interval, in seconds.
.TP
\-n COUNT
Print this many top locks, by total wait and hold time.
.TP
\-s DEPTH
Print this many stack frames for each lock. Default is 1.
.TP
\-m MIN_US
Only count acquisitions that had to wait at least this many microseconds.
glibc fires the mutex probes on every lock call, including the uncontended
fast path, so this is the usual way to look at real contention. Hold times
are always recorded, so the two blocks can have different counts.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Trace how long a process spends waiting on its mutexes:
#
.B ulockstat -p 123 -d 5
.TP
Trace only a single thread:
#
.B ulockstat -t 4567
.SH OVERHEAD
This traces all mutex lock and unlock events of the traced process and
collects a user stack trace for every contended lock. On a lock-heavy
process this can be significant, so prefer short runs and, when possible,
a PID or TID filter.
.SH SOURCE
This is from bcc.
.IP
https://github.com/iovisor/bcc
.SH OS
Linux
